WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:02.900
- [Instructor] So we have
four numbers listed here,

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.260
and what I would like you to do

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.180
is get out some pencil and
paper and pause this video

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.160
and see if you can order these numbers

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.400
from least to greatest.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.220
So the least would be at the left,

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.820
and then keep going greater, and greater,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.310
and greater until you get
to the greatest number.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:18.260
So pause the video and have a go at that.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.960
All right, now let's tackle this together.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.100
And the way that I like to do it

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.220
is I start at the, I guess you could say,

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.010
the most significant place value

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.520
or the largest place
value, compare the numbers.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.180
And then keep moving to the right

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.360
to smaller and smaller place values.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.200
So we can start in the ones place.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.670
This number has zero ones,
this number has zero ones,

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:38.370
this number has zero ones,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.360
and that number has zero ones.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.040
So the ones place really
doesn't help us much.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.030
But then we can move to the tenths place.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.430
This number has 7/10.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.630
This number has 0/10,

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:52.890
so just from that we know
that the second number

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.140
is less than the first number.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.740
This has 7/10, this has 0/10.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:57.880
It doesn't matter what's happening

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.290
in the places after that,
to the right of that.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:02.540
This number over here also has 7/10,

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:03.830
just like the first number.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.530
And this last number also has 7/10.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.260
So we know from comparing the
ones and then the tenths place

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.060
is that this number right over here

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.380
is the smallest of the four numbers.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:18.410
They all have zero ones,
but this one also has 0/10.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:23.290
So I'll list that here, 0.074.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:26.400
Now let's move to the hundredths place.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.030
So this number has 0/100.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:30.870
We've already used this number.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.050
This number has 7/100.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.185
And then this number,
it might not be obvious,

00:01:36.185 --> 00:01:39.290
but the hundredths place
you can view as being zero,

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:42.010
the hundredths place, you
can just put a zero there

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:42.843
and not change the value.

00:01:42.843 --> 00:01:45.120
So this also has 0/100.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.410
So these three numbers,
same ones, same tenths,

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:54.410
but this number, 0.77, has 7/100,

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.380
while the other two had 0/100.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.420
So this is going to be the
largest of our four numbers.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.590
This is larger than these other two

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.150
because of what we see
in the hundredths place.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.430
It doesn't matter what's happening

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:09.200
in the thousandths place
or anything beyond that.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.500
So we put the 0.77 right over there.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.200
And now we are tasked, and
we've used this number,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.530
and now we have to
compare these two numbers,

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.210
which were equal in the ones,
tenths, and hundredths place,

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:24.410
so we have to go to the thousandths place.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.920
This number has 7/1000.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.150
This number has 0/1000.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:35.150
So this number is smaller
than this first number here.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.630
So I'll write this next, 0.7,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.730
and then the third smallest,

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:43.270
or the next to largest
number is this one over here,

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:47.390
0.707, and we're done.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.360
So the main idea is you wanna compare

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.860
the most significant place values,

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.550
the largest places values first,

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.390
and then based on that,
keep moving to the right

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:59.653
to compare less and less
significant place values.

